  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Project Brief**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the user\'s request, focusing on the core technologies: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and Tailwind CSS. My main goal is to fully understand the scope, focusing on the cyborg-themed landing page as a critical starting point. I\'m prioritizing the overall structure, with an eye toward responsiveness and the later integration of 3D and parallax effects.\n\n\n**Structuring Task Separation**\n\nI\'ve realized the need for clear task isolation. I need to create separate modules or tabs for each deliverable to easily record demo videos. This includes a top-level navigation system for the user to switch between tasks and a showcase hub for an overview. This will be the main point for all deliverables. I also want to make sure the structure for each will be modular and standalone.\n\n\n**Defining Task Architecture**\n\nI\'m now solidifying the directory structure: `Task1CyborgLanding`, `Task2BuildIn3D`, `Task3ParallaxScroll`, and `Task4IdeateLanding` will be our core modules. I\'m prioritizing an in-app \"Task Code Inspector\" to showcase clean code snippets, perfect for demos, and also thinking about quick, single-click full-screen modes for each task. Finally, I\'m checking existing packages and considering `three` and `@types/three` for Task 2\'s 3D rendering, or perhaps `@react-three/fiber`.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Core Libraries**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the libraries for 3D rendering. I prefer the stability of pure `three.js` with React hooks or Canvas over `@react-three/fiber` to avoid React 19 versioning issues. The control it gives is unmatched for detailed cyborg component building, lighting, and interactive elements. I\'ve decided to install `three` and `@types/three` directly.\n\nI\'m also preparing for asset generation. Phase 1 will focus on creating high-fidelity images: a cyborg hero portrait, a neural core, and a deep cityscape parallax background, all designed for the project.\n\n\n"
